// GENERATED by scripts/blockyWorlds/buildReferenceTexts.mjs from reference/blocky-worlds/analysis/<id>/transcript.json.
// Do not edit by hand. The reference transcripts (broad.js RECIPE_REFERENCES) as plain narration text, so the edge
// function can put them into the hybrid writer's cached examples block.
import { RECIPE_REFERENCES } from "./broad.js";

export const REFERENCE_TEXTS = Object.freeze({
  "7698966230471953677": "In this town, every house has a white chalk line drawn around it. You see, after the sun goes down, anything outside a line belongs to the collector. Dogs sleep in chalk circles, cars get parked in little chalk boxes. The pizza guy stops delivering at 6, because a chalk line makes a house, and nothing can cross it. Not in and not out. Every night, my dad redraws our line, and every night, he puts a piece of chalk in my pocket. Always keep one on you, bud, until one night a storm hit, and I watched our line wash away. Dad ran outside with his chalk, but the rain melted it in his hand. Then the rain stopped, and the street lights started going out. One by one by one, then I heard it, a long wet scrape. Something stepped out of the dark, taller than the street lights. Arms dragging on the road, and it was walking straight toward my dad. So I ran outside, I pulled the chalk out of my pocket, but I didn't draw a circle around my dad. I drew it around the collector, I ran around its legs, dragging the chalk across the wet road. It turned, it reached down for me, and I closed the circle. Its hand slammed into nothing, like a wall, because a chalk line makes a house, and nothing can cross it. Not in and not out. The next morning, the town built a roof over it, so the rain could never touch it again. Now kids in our town play outside after dark, and in the middle of our street, something taller than the street lights stands perfectly still. In a house, it can never leave.",
  "7699012496337685774": "In this world, everyone is born with wings, except me. You see, nobody here walks anywhere. Our whole town is built on the side of a giant canyon. No stairs, no bridges, no roads. Babies take their first flight before their first step. The mailman drops letters straight down your chimney and grandmas race each other to the grocery store. There's only one thing that grounds everyone, rain. Wet wings get too heavy to fly, so when it rains, the whole town just sits inside and waits, except me. Since I can't fly, I climb everywhere. I've got rope burns on both hands and I carry a rope coil on my shoulder like a backpack. The other kids think it's hilarious. Hey, rope boy, need a lift? But my best friend, Nia, never laughed. Nia was the fastest flyer in our grade, and every day, she'd land on the cliff path and walk next to me. Flying's overrated, she'd say. She was lying, but it was nice. Rainy days were my favorite. The only days I wasn't last. Until one day, our class took a field trip to the bottom of the canyon. Everyone flew down. My teacher carried me in a basket, like groceries. On the way back up, the sky turned black. Then it started to pour. Everybody land! Our teacher yelled. She barely made it to the top with my basket. Kids dropped onto ledges all along the cliff. Heavy, soaked, stuck. And then I heard Nia scream. She was flying the highest, and her wings got wet the fastest. She dropped like a stone and hit a tiny ledge, halfway down the cliff wall. My wing! She cried. I can't move it! Up top, the whole town had come out. Parents, neighbors, her dad. Hundreds of people, all with wings, and not one of them could fly down to her. Her dad tried to take off. His soaked wings couldn't even lift him off the ground. Then, the ledge under Nia cracked. Little rocks started falling into the canyon. Everyone up top went silent, and I realized I was the only one who could get to her. I tied my rope around a rock, and I started climbing down. The wall was slick. My fingers kept slipping. Lightning flashed. The whole cliff shook, but I knew every crack in that wall. I'd climbed it a thousand times on a thousand rainy days. Hold on, Nia! I reached her just as the ledge cracked again. Climb on my back! I'm too heavy! My wings are soaked! Then, hold on tight! She wrapped her arms around my neck, and her heavy, wet wings hung over us like a blanket. I grabbed the rope, and I climbed. One hand, then the other. My arms were shaking. My feet kept sliding. Behind us, the ledge broke off and fell. We never heard it hit the bottom. Leave me! Nia yelled. Just go! Nope. I said, Flying's overrated. Every crack. Every rock. Every rainy day I spent out here alone, until my hands slapped onto the top of the cliff. Her dad grabbed us both and pulled us over the edge, and the whole town just stood there in the rain, staring at the kid with no wings. Then someone started clapping, and then everyone was. Everyone in this town has wings, but now, when it rains, they call me.",
  "7699174768242642190": "In this town, we all live inside a giant whale. You see, our whole town sits in its belly. Our streets run between its ribs, and wherever the whale swims, we go with it. It rains every time the whale takes a drink. Fishermen don't need boats. They just stand by the throat with nets and catch whatever it swallows. And when the whale yawns, the whole town grabs onto the yawn poles on every corner. Or you go flying. Every morning, the whale comes up to breathe. Its mouth opens, sunlight pours in, and fresh air rushes through the town. That's our sunrise. My mom is the whale keeper. She listens to its heartbeat with a stethoscope the size of a tuba. And once a year, on sneeze day, she climbs up into the nose and tickles it with a giant feather duster. The whale sneezes so hard, it blasts a whole year of junk out the blow hole like a cannon. In the middle of town, stuck deep in a rib for a hundred years, is the old harpoon. Taller than a house. Something tried to take our whale once. Mom told me, it never came back. Until one night, the whole town tilted. Houses slid. People screamed and grabbed the yawn poles. And the whale let out a sound I'd never heard before. A long, deep cry. Then, something squeezed the whale from the outside. The ribs creaked. The walls bent inward. And slowly, we started sinking. Down and down. The lanterns started going out, one street at a time. Mom pressed her stethoscope to the wall. Then she stepped back. It's back. She whispered, the thing from the deep. It's dragging us down. And if the whale can't get up for air, then I heard my best friend Kai yelling from the top of the town. Guys, come look! We climbed up to the base of the nose and looked up through the blow hole. And something looked back. An eye, bigger than our house. Yellow, pressed right up against the blow hole, staring down at our town. Long tentacles were wrapped around the whale like ropes. And I had an idea. Mom, I said, how hard does the whale sneeze? She looked at me. Then at the old harpoon. And she understood. Me, Kai, and Mom ripped the harpoon out of the rib. We dragged it all the way up into the nose and we jammed it in. Point first, aimed straight up the blow hole, right at the eye. The air was so thin now. Mom's hands were shaking. She handed me the feather duster. You do it. She said, I can't reach anymore. I climbed up next to the harpoon. The eye was right there. So close I could see myself in it. It moved closer and I tickled. The whole whale shook. Ah! The tentacles squeezed tighter. Ah! Choo! The blow hole blasted open and the old harpoon shot out like a rocket. Straight up, right into the eye. Something screamed. The whole ocean shook. The tentacles let go. And the whale was free. It rocketed up toward the surface, faster than it ever had in its life. We burst out of the water and the whale opened its mouth. Sunlight, fresh air, pouring into the town. Lanterns flickered back on. The whole town cheered and deep down below us. Something huge sank back into the dark and it never came back. The old harpoon is gone now. In its place, stuck deep in the rib, is a giant feather duster. Subscribe to support my stories.",
  "7699660040180960526": "In this world, some people are players. The rest of us are NPCs. You see, players have names floating over their heads. They jump everywhere. They steal from shops. They crash cars into buildings. And when they die, they just respawn. NPCs don't get names. We stand in one spot, and we say one line, forever. I sell hot dogs on Main Street. Nice day for a hot dog! I've said it 10,000 times a day for 11 years. Across the street is the Lemonade Girl. Lemonade! 50 cents! I've never talked to her. I can't. But every morning, when the server starts, she says her line first. And it's my favorite part of the day. The only person NPCs are scared of is the admin. Once, I saw him. A man with a golden crown holding a glowing hammer. A player was cheating, flying through walls. And the admin swung that hammer and said one word. BANNED! The player turned into pixels and vanished. Then the admin walked back to the grey building at the edge of the map, and we never saw him again. Until one morning, a new player joined. His name floated above his head in red. XX destroyer XX. He didn't buy anything. He didn't play. He pulled out a glowing purple gun, and he pointed it at the mailman. ZAP! The mailman turned into pixels, and he didn't respawn. Then the crossing guard. Then the ice cream truck. Everyone just stood there, saying their lines. Because that's all we can do. Then he pointed the gun at me. ZAP! Everything went fuzzy. My body flickered. But I didn't disappear. Something was different. I tried to say my line. Nice day for a... for a... HELP! I said a new word for the first time in 11 years. And then I took a step, off my spot. I was glitched, and I was free. Then I heard it, across the street. Lemonade! 50 cents! He was pointing the gun at her. I ran, faster than I knew I could, and I tackled her out of the way. ZAP! Her lemonade stand turned to pixels, but she didn't. Where I'd grabbed her arm, it flickered, just like mine. She stared at me. Lemonade? 50 cents? Come on! I said, I know who can stop him. We ran to the edge of the map. There was an invisible wall there. No NPC had ever gone through it. But we were glitched. We pushed, and we fell right through. The grey building. The admins building. And sitting inside, on a table, glowing, was the hammer. Behind us, red letters floated through the door. XX destroyer XX. He pointed his gun at the lemonade girl. I grabbed the hammer. It was so heavy. But I swung it with everything I had. And I said the word. BANNED! He froze. Then he turned into pixels, and he vanished. Forever. At midnight, the server restarted. The mailman came back. The ice cream truck came back. And I woke up behind my hot dog cart. Back on my spot. Nice day for a hot dog! Just like always. Then, from across the street, nice day. For a hot dog? I looked up. The lemonade girl waved at me. She remembered. And the hammer? It's under my cart now. So if you ever play in our town, be nice to the hot dog guy.",
  "7699870521722211598": "In this village, every family leaves one plate of dinner on the porch, every night, for the great beast. You see, our village sits at the edge of the black woods, and every night, something comes out of those trees. Nobody has ever seen it, we just hear it. Heavy footsteps, slow breathing, porch boards creaking, and by morning, every plate is licked clean. There's only one rule, never forget the plate. Thirty years ago, the Marsh family disappeared. Everyone says the great beast took them. Their house at the end of the road is still empty. Doors open, beds made, nobody's been inside since. At my house, the plate is my job, because my mom goes to bed every night right at sunset. She says she's a morning person, but she never eats breakfast. She's never hungry, and her fingernails are always chipped. Gardening, she says, until one week, mom got a fever that wouldn't break, and on the third night, I forgot the plate. At 3 a.m., I woke up to a sound. Scratch, scratch, not the front door, the back door, right next to mom's room. The doorknob turned, and then it opened. Heavy footsteps, down the hall. In the morning, I checked every porch in the village. Every plate was still full, and when I brought mom her soup, there was mud on her blanket, and long scratches down her arms. Rosebushes, she said, and rolled over. The next night, the great beast didn't come, or the next, but something else did. Gardens torn up, fences knocked down, and at night, out in the black woods, green eyes, hundreds of them. And I had to know what the great beast really was, so I went to the Marsh House, and on the fireplace, there was a photo. A little girl standing in the woods, hugging the paw of something enormous, covered in black fur. On the back, someone had written, our daughter, age 12, her first night. I knew that girl. It was my mom. The Marsh family never got taken by the great beast. They were the great beast. For 30 years, every plate on every porch had been feeding the one thing, keeping those green eyes away. My mom. That's why she goes to bed at sunset. That's why she's never hungry for breakfast. And the night I forgot her plate, she went out to fight anyway. Sick and starving, and she lost. I ran home. The green eyes were already in the streets. Hundreds of them, scratching at doors. Mom was trying to get out of bed. Stay inside. She whispered, I'll handle it. But she couldn't even stand. Then I looked at the photo again. Age 12. Her first night. I'm 12. I walked outside. And I went porch to porch. Meatloaf. Soup. Pizza. A whole cake. I ate every plate on our street. And then I felt it. My hands got bigger. My back cracked. Black fur came rushing down my arms. I grew. And grew. Taller than the houses. And I let out a roar that shook every window in town. Every single green eye ran back into the woods. Mom got better that week. The village still leaves their plates out every night. Nobody knows the truth. But now, at sunset, two shapes walk into the black woods. And every morning, mom asks me if I want breakfast. I'm never hungry.",
  "7700821616963374350": "In my world, everyone gets a random superpower on the worst day of their life. You see, nobody knows what power you'll get. Or when. Your worst day just shows up. And so does your power. The lady whose house flooded? She can breathe underwater now. The mailman who got chased by 12 dogs in one day? Now he can outrun a car. Whenever your power finally shows up, you go to the power office. They scan you, and a screen tells you exactly what it is. And the best ones get picked to join the Hero Academy. My best friend Jake got the best power in the world. On the day his dog ran away, he started glowing. Now he can fly. He shoots light from his hands. He's the youngest hero in history. Captain Shine. And me? I'm the kid standing next to Captain Shine in every picture. Slightly cut off. Nobody ever asks for my autograph. They ask me to take the picture. Until one day. My birthday. I'd been planning my party for months. Jake showed up an hour late. Flying. With a news crew. Everybody ran over to him. Then he tried to light my birthday candles with a light blast. And he set my whole house on fire. The fire department came. Everybody got out. And the next morning, the newspaper said, Captain Shine saves birthday party from fire. My house was gone. And he got a parade. That was the worst day of my life. And that night, I felt it. Something cold. Right in my chest. My power. The next morning, I went to the power office. They put me in the scanner. And the screen said, Unknown. The scientists scanned me again. And again. Unknown. Sorry, kid. They said, Maybe it's nothing. But weird things started happening. Every time I got mad, The street lights on our block flickered. Every time Captain Shine came on TV, The TV went black. My night light kept dying. Mom bought five new ones. All dead. Nobody knew what my power was. Not even me. Then came the hero parade. For Captain Shine. Downtown. Half a million people. Fireworks. Spotlights. And Jake flying over the crowd. Glowing brighter than the sun. He landed in front of me. Put his arm around me. And posed for the cameras. Everybody! This is my best friend! He gets a free hot dog! Everybody laughed. And that cold feeling in my chest got colder. Then, one by one, The spotlights went out. The fireworks fizzled in the sky. Every streetlight. Every building. Every phone screen. The whole city went dark. And so did Jake. His glow flickered and died. He dropped out of the sky. And splashed into the fountain. Half a million people screaming in the dark. And me, standing in the middle of it. That's when I finally understood my power. I can turn off any light. Any light. Even his. The next morning, There was a new name on the front page of the newspaper. Not Captain Shine. Mine. They called me Blackout. And for the first time in my whole life, Everybody was looking at me.",
  "7701004539062734088": "In this town, nobody whistles after sunset. You see, if you whistle in the dark, something whistles back. It always sounds exactly like you, and the closer it gets, the more familiar it becomes. At first, people thought it was just an old superstition. Then a man whistled outside his house one night, heard an answer from the forest, and followed it into the trees. Nobody ever saw him again. After that, the rule became law. My grandfather took it more seriously than anyone. He locked every window before sunset, and told me never to make a sound outside after dark. Some things don't need to know where you are, he always said. I thought he was being ridiculous. Then one night, I was walking home with my little brother, Sam, when I accidentally whistled. It was just a tune I'd heard on the radio. I stopped immediately, remembering the rule. For a few seconds, nothing happened. Then someone whistled from behind us. It was the exact same tune. Sam grabbed my sleeve. Was that you? No, we hurried home without looking back. That night, I told grandfather what had happened. He went completely pale. What direction did it come from? The forest. He locked the doors, closed the curtains, and made us sleep in his bedroom. At midnight, I woke up to a familiar sound. Three short whistles, then two long ones. It was the little tune my mother used to whistle when she cooked dinner. She passed away when I was six. I sat up in bed. Grandfather was already awake, staring at the window. Don't answer it, he whispered. The whistle came again, this time from the front porch. Then I heard my mother's voice. Sweetheart, open the door. I started crying. It sounded exactly like her. Grandfather grabbed my shoulders. That isn't your mother, the voice called again. Please, I'm cold. I wanted to run outside. I wanted to see her, even if it was only for a second. But grandfather held me still until the voice finally disappeared. In the morning, I asked him how the thing knew my mother's voice. He took me to the attic and opened an old wooden box. Inside were dozens of cassette tapes. They were recordings of my mother singing, laughing, and talking to us when we were little. Grandfather explained that the thing in the forest didn't create voices. It copied sounds it heard in town, then used them to lure people outside. The more people answered it, the better it became at imitating them. And because I had whistled first, it had learned my tune. That afternoon, grandfather burned every recording of my mother. I hated him for it. For weeks, I barely spoke to him. Then, one evening, the power went out during a storm. Grandfather went outside to fix the generator, telling me to stay inside. He never came back. I searched the yard with a flashlight until I found his tools beside the forest path. Then I heard his whistle. Three short notes. Two long ones. I almost answered. Instead, I remembered what he'd taught me. The creature copied sounds, but it couldn't understand what they meant. So I did something different. I knocked three times against the metal fence, then twice against the wooden gate. A moment later, I heard a real voice from the darkness. Sam? Is that you? It was grandfather. He'd fallen into a ditch beside the path and couldn't climb out. I followed his voice, found him, and helped him back home. We never heard the creature again. Years later, I still don't whistle after sunset. But sometimes, when the wind blows through the trees, I hear a familiar tune drifting out of the forest. I don't answer it, because some voices are worth remembering. And some are better left unheard.",
  "7701115575648455950": "In this world, every town is built on the back of a giant animal. You see, some towns live on enormous turtles. Others live on elephants, wolves, bears, and even gigantic birds. And wherever your animal goes, your entire town goes with it. The turtle towns move slowly, but they're the safest. The bird towns travel above the clouds, and their people have to tie down everything they own. And the wolf towns? Nobody goes near the wolf towns. Because giant animals don't always get along. Some are best friends. Some travel together for hundreds of years, and some have been fighting since before humans existed. I live on the back of an enormous turtle named Moss. Our town has 300 houses, a school, a hospital, and a tiny grocery store built right beside his neck. Every morning, Moss walks through the forest, eating enormous trees along the way. Moss is the gentlest animal in the entire forest, and he always goes out of his way to avoid a fight. And there's one animal he absolutely refuses to approach, an enormous black wolf named Fang. Fang carries the biggest city in the world on his back. Thousands of buildings, enormous towers, and an army of soldiers who raid smaller animal towns for supplies. Every year, Fang travels through our forest, and every year, Moss hides inside his shell until the wolf passes, until one morning. We woke up to enormous footsteps. I ran outside, and my stomach dropped. Fang was standing directly in front of us. His glowing red eyes were staring straight at Moss, and on his back, thousands of soldiers were preparing enormous ropes and metal hooks. Everyone inside! Grandpa shouted. Fang let out a deafening growl. Then, he lunged. His enormous jaws clamped around Moss's shell. The entire town shook. Houses collapsed. Trees snapped. People screamed. Moss tried retreating into his shell, but Fang's soldiers fired gigantic hooks into the edges. Holding him in place. Then, Grandpa noticed something. A massive chain was wrapped around Fang's leg, stretching up to the city on his back. Every time he tried to stop attacking, the soldiers pulled it tighter. Fang wasn't attacking willingly. He was being controlled. Moss and Fang used to be best friends. Grandpa whispered before those soldiers captured him. Suddenly, I remembered Grandpa's enormous wooden horn. He always used it to wake Moss, but he'd once told me that giant animals could recognize each other's calls. I sprinted home, grabbed the horn, and climbed onto our roof. Then I blew as hard as I could. Boo! Fang froze. Moss lifted his head and answered with a deep, rumbling call. Fang's eyes widened. He recognized his old friend. The soldiers pulled harder, but Fang refused to attack. Then Moss charged forward and clamped his enormous jaws around the chain. Snap! The chain broke. Fang stumbled backward, then gently pressed his forehead against Moss's shell. The soldiers tried attacking again, but Fang shook his body, sending their weapons flying and forcing them to surrender. That evening, the two old friends walked through the forest together. Our towns even built bridges connecting their backs. Now, whenever Moss moves too slowly and we're late for school, Fang gives him a little push, and we arrive three hours early.",
  "7701548589666798880": "In my town, you have to be home before the streetlights go out, because whoever is still outside when they do is gone by morning. Last month, that was my big brother, and last night, I found out where everyone goes. Every night at 9, the lights switch off one street at a time. It starts at the edge of town, and you can watch the dark rolling toward you, lamp by lamp. Nobody knows what's in it, because nobody who got caught ever came back to tell us. All we know is that the next morning, there's a new name on the missing wall, and a brand new streetlight on that street. The mayor says it's for safety, so nobody ever gets caught there again. My big brother Arlo thought the whole thing was a game. Every night, he raced the dark home. His orange scarf flying behind him, and slammed the door with one second left. Then, he'd flick our porch light twice. Tap, tap! I'm home! Mom hated it. I thought he was the coolest person alive. Then, one night, the dark was faster. I saw him at the corner, running and grinning like always, with the dark right behind him. Then, it was in front of him. Arlo was one house away. Our street went black, and when the lights came back on, the sidewalk was empty. The next morning, there was a new streetlight right in front of our house. Tall, shiny, and caught at the very top was an orange scarf. Everyone said the wind must have blown it up there, but I couldn't stop staring at that lamp. So, I counted every streetlight in town. Two hundred and twelve. Then, I counted the names on the missing wall. Two hundred and twelve. Every night at nine, mom closes the curtains and says, Don't look at the dark. That night, I looked. Our street went black. And then, something moved. The streetlights. They were walking. Long, thin bodies bending over the houses. Their heads turning from window to window. Then, a car came around the corner with its headlights on, and the lamp and its beam froze mid-step. It didn't move again until the car was gone. At sunrise, every lamp was standing exactly where it belonged, except ours. Ours was right outside my window, and its light flickered twice. Tap, tap. I'm home. That's when everything made sense. The streetlights don't protect us from the dark. They are the dark. They switch off so nobody can see them hunting. And the light inside every lamp is someone they took. The next night, at five to nine, I snuck outside with dad's flashlight, his hammer, and a ladder. The street went black. Our lamp turned its head toward me. I wedged the flashlight in the mailbox, pointed straight at it, and it froze. My hands were shaking so hard that every time the beam wobbled off the glass, its long arm twitched toward me. I climbed the ladder and swung the hammer as hard as I could. The glass exploded into light, and Arlo tumbled out onto the grass, blinking, still wearing half of his scarf. He looked up at me and said, Did I make it? The next night, the whole town was outside before nine. Every flashlight, every phone, every car with its high beams on. When the lights went out, two hundred and twelve lamps froze in two hundred and twelve beams. And one by one, we brought everyone home. Now our town stays lit all night, and nobody has gone missing since. But this morning, there was a brand new streetlight at the end of our street. Nobody is missing. So whose light is that?",
  "7701969278022552850": "In this world, everyone lives underground. There are cities beneath the ground, with houses, schools, shops, and even fake skies painted on the ceilings. Nobody has ever seen the real sky. People say the surface is too dangerous, the air is poisonous, the ground is burning, and anyone who goes outside will die within minutes. So, everyone follows one simple rule. Never go above ground. I was a 16-year-old boy who lived in the lowest part of the city. Unlike everyone else, I was curious about what existed above us. Every night, I would sneak into the old library and read books about the surface. They showed pictures of blue skies, green forests, and oceans stretching farther than the eye could see. But one day, I found a book with a strange message written inside. The surface is not what they told you. I thought it was a joke. Until I found a rusty ladder hidden behind a wall, it led to a locked metal door. And on that door were three words. Keep the sun out. I knew I should leave, but I had spent my whole life wondering what was up there. So, I climbed the ladder and pushed against the door. It opened. Bright light flooded my eyes, forcing me to cover my face. I had never seen anything so bright before. When my eyes finally adjusted, I stopped breathing. The sky was blue. Grass covered the ground, and birds flew between the trees. The surface wasn't dangerous at all. But then, I noticed something strange. There were no roads, no houses, no people. Only one enormous structure standing in the distance. A tower. I walked toward it and found a glass window. Inside were hundreds of screens showing our underground cities. Every street, every house, every person they were watching us. Suddenly, a speaker above the window crackled. Unauthorized citizen detected. My heart nearly stopped. Then, a voice spoke again. Subject has reached the surface. Begin the reset. A loud alarm rang from beneath my feet. I looked down and saw the ground split open. Huge metal panels began rising from underground, covering the grass and trees. The beautiful world was being buried. I ran back toward the door, but a voice stopped me. Wait! An old man stepped out from behind the tower. You need to know the truth, he said. The surface was never destroyed. Your leaders moved everyone underground because they wanted complete control. Down there, they control what you see, what you learn, and what you believe. I stared at him. Then why are they covering the surface? The old man looked toward the sky because people are starting to remember. Suddenly, every screen in the tower went black. Then, a message appeared across the underground cities. The surface is safe. For the first time in history, millions of people looked up at their fake skies. And one by one, they began climbing toward the light. But as I watched them emerge, I noticed something terrifying. The old man was gone. In his place stood a young boy wearing my exact clothes. He looked at me and smiled. You made it, he said. Now it's my turn to go underground.",
  "7702135684059909384": "In this world, everyone lives inside a massive supermarket. The shelves stretch so far that nobody can see the end. People build houses out of furniture displays, use shopping carts as vehicles, and grow food in the produce section. Nobody knows how big the supermarket really is. Once you enter, finding the exit becomes almost impossible. But there's one rule everyone follows. When the lights go out, find shelter! During the day, the employees look completely normal. They smile at customers and help people find what they need. But when the lights turn off, everything changes. Their smiles disappear, their faces become blank, and they begin searching the aisles for anyone still outside. And if they find you, they won't stop following you. I was a delivery boy who lived inside a furniture store with my family. Every day, I traveled through different aisles to collect food and supplies. Our shelter was safe, and we had enough food to survive. Until one night, my little sister failed to return before closing time. I grabbed a flashlight and ran outside to look for her. The lights flickered. Then, everything went dark. From somewhere in the distance, I heard a man's voice. Customer service! I froze. A tall employee stood at the end of the aisle. His uniform was torn, his arms hung unnaturally low, and his face had no expression. Then, he turned toward me. How can I help you? I ran. More employees emerged from the darkness. Some crawled across the floor, while others climbed over furniture displays to reach me. I hid behind a sofa and held my breath. Then, I heard a familiar voice. Brother? It was my sister. She was hiding inside a wardrobe just a few feet away. I grabbed her hand and we ran. But an employee suddenly stepped in front of us. He tilted his head. You forgot something. I looked behind him. Dozens of employees were blocking the aisle. We were trapped. Then, my sister pointed toward a nearby display bed. We climbed onto the mattress and pulled the blankets over ourselves. The employees searched every corner, but none of them noticed us. We stayed completely still until the lights came back on. When morning arrived, the employees returned to normal. They smiled, straightened their uniforms, and walked away as if nothing had happened. We returned to our shelter, but my father was waiting at the entrance. He looked terrified. Where have you two been? I explained everything, including the strange employee who had chased us. My father's face went pale. Then, he pointed toward the wall. Someone had written a message there overnight. They are learning your hiding places. Suddenly, the supermarket speakers crackled. Attention, customers! Everyone froze. We have a new employee starting today. At the end of the aisle, a familiar figure appeared. It was the employee who had chased us. But this time, he wasn't wearing a uniform. He was wearing my father's clothes, and he was smiling.",
  "7703332734629924128": "In this town, the richer you are, the higher your house is. You see, poor people live all the way down here, in tiny wooden houses. People with a little more money live on small towers, maybe 10 floors high. Then, you have the rich families. Their houses are hundreds of floors above the ground, with giant pools, basketball courts, and private elevators. But the billionaires? Huh, their houses are so high, they literally reach outer space. Some even have swimming pools floating next to the moon. And me? Well, this is my house. A tiny wooden box right next to the garbage dump. Every morning, I watch rich kids take private elevators to school, while I have to climb a broken ladder just to reach the sidewalk. My best friend lives on floor 200. He has his own basketball court, a giant pool, and even a pet dinosaur. Last week, he invited me over, but the security guard took one look at my clothes and kicked me out. Apparently, ground level people aren't allowed above the clouds. But everything changed one morning. I woke up to a massive earthquake. The ground was shaking. People were screaming. And then, boom! A gigantic golden mansion fell straight out of the sky, right into the empty lot next to my house. Golden walls, diamond windows, and a swimming pool bigger than my entire neighborhood. Thousands of people gathered around it, but nobody dared to go inside. Then, I noticed something strange. A little golden key was lying right in front of my door. I picked it up, and suddenly, the mansion's front doors opened. A giant screen turned on, and a robotic voice said, New owner identified. My face appeared on every screen in the city. Even the billionaires in space could see me. Then, the voice said something that made everyone freeze. Warning, the previous owner did not fall. He was pushed, and suddenly, every mansion above the clouds started moving, straight toward me.",
  "7703429226372009249": "In this town, the richer you are, the higher your house is. I used to live next to a garbage dump, until a golden mansion fell from the sky and a mysterious key made me its new owner. And now, every billionaire mansion in the sky is moving straight toward me. They're getting closer. Suddenly, the golden key in my hand starts glowing. A secret elevator rises from the ground. I jump inside and before I can press a button, it shoots into the sky. I fly past the middle class towers, rooftop pools, and my rich friend's pet dinosaur. Floor 500, floor 1,000, floor 10,000. I'm above the clouds. But the elevator keeps going until I reach a gigantic golden castle floating in space. Inside, the richest billionaire in the world is waiting for me. The moment he sees my key, his face turns pale. That key was supposed to be destroyed! Behind him, I discover a massive machine covered in glowing buttons. Every button controls a house in my town. And that's when I realize the truth. The billionaires have been controlling the entire city. They decide who lives above the clouds and who stays down next to the garbage. The previous owner discovered their secret. He tried to shut the machine down, so the billionaires pushed his mansion out of the sky. And now, they're coming for me. The billionaire lunges at me, trying to grab my key. I dodge him, sprint across the room, and slam the key into the machine. A giant red button lights up. Reset the city. I press it. The entire castle starts shaking. Thousands of houses begin moving at once. Tiny wooden shacks rise from the ground. Giant billionaire mansions descend from the clouds. And suddenly, every family has a beautiful home at the same height. Nobody lives above anyone else anymore. For the first time, everyone is equal. But then, something unexpected happens. The billionaire's golden castle vanishes. I hear someone screaming far below. I look down. And there he is, the richest man in the world. Sitting inside my old wooden shack, right next to the garbage dump. I can't help but smile. Welcome to the ground floor!",
  "7703557612477910305": "In this town, nobody is allowed to look at the moon, not even for one second. See, my name is Kai, and I live in a small village in ancient Egypt. Every night, a giant bell rings three times. Everyone locks their doors and closes their windows, because something terrifying walks through our town. My father always warned me, whatever you hear outside, never look at the moon. But one night, I broke the rule. I slowly opened my window, and what I saw made my heart stop. Standing between the pyramids was a gigantic creature with the head of a jackal. Its golden eyes glowed in the darkness. It was Anubis, the ancient guardian of the dead. Suddenly, he stopped and turned directly toward my window. I froze, but instead of attacking, Anubis knelt before me. The next morning, I discovered a glowing golden symbol on my hand. When my father saw it, he panicked. We have to leave, now! But suddenly, the ground began shaking. The largest pyramid split open, revealing a staircase deep underground. Something was calling my name. I followed the stairs into an enormous golden throne room. Hundreds of stone monsters were kneeling. Above a giant throne, ancient writing glowed. The last pharaoh has returned. Then, I noticed a painting on the wall. A young pharaoh wearing a golden crown. He looked exactly like me. But behind him, an entire city was burning. Suddenly, my memories returned. Thousands of years ago, I was that pharaoh. I had commanded Anubis to destroy a kingdom, but I regretted my decision. So I sacrificed my powers to save everyone, and erased my memories. Anubis had guarded the village ever since. Not to hurt us, but to prevent my powers from returning. Then, Anubis appeared before the throne. He bowed his head, waiting for my command. I could reclaim my power, or destroy the curse forever. I stepped toward the throne, grabbed the golden crown, and shattered it. The entire pyramid began collapsing. Anubis slowly turned into golden dust. When I finally escaped, the sun was rising. For the first time in thousands of years, nobody had to fear the moon. The curse was finally broken.",
  "7703625168987475233": "In this town the weather follows your mood. Everybody has their own little sky floating over their head. Our mailman is in love so he walks around under a rainbow. My teacher on a Monday thunderstorm and when my baby cousin throws a tantrum we all run inside. That's a tornado. The mayor is so happy he has his own personal son he has never worn a jacket. Then there's me. I'm the grumpiest kid in town. Over my head it rains every single day. At school they call me drizzle. Nobody sits next to me at lunch because their sandwiches get soggy. Nobody except Mia. Mia lives next door and every morning she waits at the gate with her yellow umbrella. She holds it over me the whole way to school and she never complains. Your cloud is kind of cute she says. It is not cute. Then came the summer of the big heat wave. 40 days not one drop of rain. The lake turned into a puddle. The puddle turned into dust. The grown-ups tried everything. The fire trucks sprayed the fields until their tanks ran dry. The mayor did a rain dance in front of the whole town but everybody was way too happy. Sun, sun, more sun. Then the weatherman pointed at me. There's only one cloud left in this town. Everybody turned around. Drizzle said the mayor. We need you grumpy. Three days no smiling. So the whole town got to work. My mom made broccoli for breakfast. My teacher gave me a pop quiz. On a Saturday the mailman stepped on my toe twice and my cloud got bigger and darker. But there was one problem. Mia. Every time she walked by I almost smiled. So on day three Mia did the hardest thing ever. She closed her umbrella and she stayed away. That was the grumpiest I have ever been. My cloud cracked with thunder and it poured over the fields, over the lake, over the whole town. Everybody danced in the rain. And when it was over Mia came running with her yellow umbrella. I'm so proud of your bad mood. She said. Now everyone in town is super nice to me. It's completely ruining my weather.",
  "7703675002629000481": "In this town Santa only lands on one house every Christmas the house with the brightest lights from there He delivers to everyone so every December this town goes completely crazy Mr.. Henderson put a stadium floodlight on his roof the parkers have a reindeer that shoots lasers and Grandma Rose has 40,000 light strings airplanes use her house to land then there's us our house has one light bulb one Sad bulb at school. They call me light bulb every year Santa flies right over our house without even looking But my grandpa doesn't care he gave me his old flashlight the kind with a crank It doesn't need power. He said just patience I keep it under my pillow then came this Christmas Eve the biggest snowstorm in a hundred years at 8 o'clock The power went out in the whole town the floodlight died the laser reindeer died grandma roses house went black The grown-ups ran for their generators But they were frozen solid and somewhere above the clouds we heard sleigh bells going round and round Santa was lost no lights no landing no presence for anyone the little kids on our street started crying Then I remembered I grabbed grandpa's flashlight and climbed up onto our roof and I cranked and cranked my arm burned My fingers froze just patience. I whispered one tiny light the only light in the whole town And then out of the storm came eight reindeer and a big red sleigh. They landed right on our roof Santa looked at my flashlight then at me best light I've seen all year he said that night every present in town was delivered from our tiny house Grandpa just smiled told you patience and the next year everybody in town bought one sad bulb you",
  "7704068720796208406": "In this town, everyone has a sniper targeting them at all times. Nobody knows where they're hiding, but everyone has a little red laser dot following them everywhere. And if you break even one rule, you get shot. Yesterday, I was sitting in math's class when my friend refused to do his homework. His laser dot started flashing. Suddenly, a paintball flew through the window and hit him right in the forehead. He fell straight off his chair. Everyone just carried on working. Apparently, that's normal here. Later, I got hungry and started eating my lunch five minutes early. Big mistake! My laser turned bright red. Before I could even swallow, bang! A paintball hit me straight in the face. Then I watched my dad get shot for wearing socks with sandals, and the school bully got shot for cutting in line. Honestly, these snipers were getting ridiculous. But the next morning, something strange happened. I woke up and my laser dot was gone. Everyone started panicking. Nobody had ever lost their laser before. I looked up at the tallest building in town, and there he was, my sniper, packing up his equipment. He held up a giant sign that said, I quit. I couldn't believe it for the first time in my life. I was free, or so I thought, because suddenly, a new red laser appeared on my forehead. I slowly turned around, and standing behind me was my little brother holding a giant toy sniper rifle. He looked me dead in the eyes and shouted, I got promoted."
});

/** The examples for ids, in the given order: [{ id, text, firstLine }]; unknown ids are skipped. */
export function examplesByIds(ids) {
  return (ids ?? []).filter((id) => REFERENCE_TEXTS[id]).map((id) => ({ id, text: REFERENCE_TEXTS[id], firstLine: REFERENCE_TEXTS[id].split(/(?<=[.!?])\s+/)[0] }));
}

/** The examples a recipe learns from (best first). The same recipe always gives the same list: a stable cached prefix. */
export const examplesFor = (recipeId) => examplesByIds(RECIPE_REFERENCES[recipeId] ?? []);
